    <title>Stanford CS329Z: Engineering AI Agents (Course Guide)</title>
    <link href="https://songchen.science/blog/posts/95ef431f/"/>
    <id>https://songchen.science/blog/posts/95ef431f/</id>
    <published>2026-11-06T10:26:46.000Z</published>
    <updated>2026-11-06T10:35:04.463Z</updated>
    
    
    <summary type="html">&lt;a class=&quot;tag is-dark is-medium&quot; href=&quot;https://cs329z.stanford.edu/&quot;&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;icon&quot;&gt;&lt;i class=&quot;fa fa-book&quot;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Stanford CS329Z&lt;/a&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A guide to &lt;a href=&quot;https://cs329z.stanford.edu/&quot;&gt;Stanford CS329Z: Engineering AI Agents&lt;/a&gt;, written in my own words from the course website as it stood on 6 November 2026. The slides, assignments and course text belong to the CS329Z teaching team; follow the links for the originals. The schedule is tentative and may change during the quarter.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CS329Z is a 3-unit Stanford course for Autumn 2026, taught by &lt;a href=&quot;https://cs.stanford.edu/~diyiy/&quot;&gt;Diyi Yang&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;https://michryan.com/&quot;&gt;Michael Ryan&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;https://john-b-yang.github.io/&quot;&gt;John Yang&lt;/a&gt;. It is about building software around language models: from a single prompted call to pipelines, then to compound systems that combine models with retrievers, tools and optimizers, and finally to agents that choose their own next step.&lt;/p&gt;</summary>
    
    
    
